A||N||6||GT-AutoX||Everything I fix makes it worse!||||1||
Z||000000||GT-AutoX||05-28-2001||12:44 PM||||Greetings, what a great wealth of information you have here.  This is my first post.<P>Just bought an 86.5 GT 5spd with 97K from a lot and it's looking great in my driveway, I just wish it ran.  Test drove fine (belt squealer on startup), started it multiple times etc., bought it, drove it 10 miles to get home, let it cool down and then it wouldn't restart.  No spark, changed all ingnition components until finally a new pick-up coil fixed the problem.  The distributor had to come out but I marked the position, so my timing should be close.<P>Went to an autocross, did okay except it almost overheated with that belt problem (doesn't like to sit idling in a long cue so the temp guage got near red at one point before I got to run which brought it back down to normal), anyway even before that episode I noticed some engine hesitation and popping right after I start it which went away after a few revs.  Drove it 20 miles home from aforementioned auto-x with no problems, no hesitation, no overheating, etc.  Drove it around town once more and the hesitation got worse.  Warm-up wasn't helping, idle roughened, timing adjustments did not help, so I checked the codes and went looking for vacuum leaks.  Got a code 32 (egr function) Plugged one at the EGR solenoid and replaced my cracked egr tube, coat-hangered egr valve etc.  Started it and then the idle got REALLY rough.  Now it won't start and is giving me code 32 and code 34 (MAP sensor).<P>In Summary -- <BR>Symptoms: Now won't start, fires up for a second or two but won't rev or idle.  Also smells a little like gas back there when trying to start  My timing light gets pulses and the entire ignition system is new & checked for continuity so I think I'm getting spark.  The fuel pressure schrader valve squirts gas when I push it, so gas is getting pumped.<P>Parts I've replaced (all delco): Cap&Rotor, Ignition Module, Ignition coil, Pick-up coil, ECM, EGR valve, EGR tube, oil& filter.<P>Where I'm at: Intake Plenum is off.  Checked all vacuum lines for leaks or blockages, Checked EGR solenoid and got a minimum resistance of 1 Megaohm between any two terminals and it does nothing when I apply 12V, so it's probably shot but would that really prevent it from starting?  Same with the MAP, but that code came on after it went downhill and could that prevent it from starting?  Besides it won't start even with the ALDL grounded.  Plug wires look new, spark plugs were supposedly done by the dealer and they look good.<P>Anyway right now I'm going out to test the TPS and take another look at the plugs, They were getting wet the last time I checked.<P>Any suggestions?  I'm kind of desperate because I just lost 3rd + 4th gears in the Nissan and I need to get to work tomorrow (with "pride" I hope).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||BAM-BAM||05-28-2001||01:06 PM||||  Welcome and sorry to hear your delima. My 85GT was extremely problematic until I found this Forum. You are right, there is a wealth of information here. As for your starting problem, it sounds similar to what I went through. It seems like the more you fix the worse it gets. What I did eventualy was replaced all my ECM sensors, plugs (gapped myself) wires, cap rotor and so on. I also had vacuum leakes from the EGR being crushed and the EGR vacuum solenoid was missing. After replacing all of the aforementioned components, It started right up and has ran great ever since. Now I'm not saying that you will have to do that much or that I really had to do that much. Rather, I was tired of the, let's try this one, method. At this point I would say that your plug wires are the root of the problem. But to be on the safe side and the fact that the car is over 15 years old, just shotgun the whole system. That way you won't have to worry about a different problem a month from now. That's just my opinion. You'll find also, that you have an excellent car that will be very dependable. Fiero's, you either love 'em<BR>or you hate 'em. Hope that was helpful, good luck!<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>P.S. It can only be one of two things; Electrical system or fuel delivery. Sounds like you are getting fuel (rail pressure, wet plugs)So look to the electrical, start with the plugs and wires and work your way back. Did you heatsink the ignition module, is the distributor locked down? things like that.<P>PEACE!!<P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||12
Z||000002||Taijiguy||05-28-2001||01:31 PM||||You indicated the problem got progressively worse until it would run no more. Any chance the distrubutor was loose enough to allow it to move? Could it have turned on you? Just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Iron Duke Wellingsley||05-28-2001||08:42 PM||||It sounds like a fuel pump or maybe a fuel filter.<P>I would get a fuel pressure gauge and confirm that it's getting enough pressure.  As for the overheating, does your fan turn on?  It should turn on long before you get to the red zone on the gauge.<P>IF you have an A/C car, then switch on the A/C and the fan should run (even if the A/C doesn't work).  <P>Is your coolant 50/50 antifreeze/water?  If it's pure anything, then the car will overheat.  <P>When was the thermostat replaced?  If it is not working properly (partially stuck shut) then your car will overheat unpredictably.<P>It's an old car, so don't assume that only one part is broken.  The more likely condition is that a number of parts are near the end of their useful life.  Keep your eyes open and be thorough.  A reliable Fiero is one that is well maintained.<P>On the positive side, the Fiero has huge potential to perform in autocross.  The handling of these cars is quite good stock and with some $$$$ you can get well more than 1G lateral acceleration on street-legal tires.<P>Have fun, it's more than a car . . . it's your hobby and a direct link to your finances.  Just hold your wallet open and smile.  Soon, you will be pulling amazing Gs!||||reg||6
Z||000004||Raydar||05-28-2001||10:15 PM||||Don't know if this will help or not...<BR>After I replaced my broken EGR tube, my Formula wouldn't idle. Could barely keep it running. Had to keep my foot on the gas, constantly. I'm guessing this was caused by the IAC being all the way closed, due to the ECM trying to slow the high idle caused by the EGR tube leak. Once I drove the car for a few minutes, the ECM was able to adjust the idle correctly. As I understand it, you have to drive over 35 MPH, or something like that, to get the IAC to reset. The more I drove it over the next few days, the better the idle quality got.<BR>I know this doesn't address all of your questions, but maybe it'll be useful. <BR>Welcome to the Forum!<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jay||05-28-2001||11:45 PM||||Since you've done the work on the car have you cleared the codes from the ECM by disconnecting the battery?  This will give you the current codes and should clear out the ones that are stored in it's memory.<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/gangster.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT-AutoX||05-29-2001||12:50 AM||||Thanks everyone.  Got it running just in time!  More details in my next topic.||||reg||6
